; exit 72 (see .exit)
; A dying program says WHICH FUNCTION died.
;
; `310-effect-unhandled` and `314-out-of-memory` pin the sentence each
; trap prints. Neither pins that anything follows it, and until
; 2026-08-24 nothing did: a status and one line, which answers what
; happened and nothing about where. The three SIGSEGVs
; `scripts/check-stack-depth.sh` records were each diagnosed by
; attaching `lldb`, and a worker dying inside a pre-forked pool has no
; `lldb` attached and no second chance.
;
; WHAT THIS CASE PINS, and what it deliberately does not. The `.err`
; golden carries the message and the backtrace's MARKER LINE, and the
; runner compares stderr only as far as that marker
; (`scripts/run-stdlib-tests.sh`). The frames themselves are not
; comparable here: which of them survive depends on what the optimiser
; inlined, that depends on the LLVM version, and the three CI legs do
; not run the same one. Pinning them in a golden would be a fixture
; that fails on a runner upgrade while the compiler is correct.
;
; So this case pins the half that is stable everywhere - a trap prints
; a trace at all, and the status is still the trap's own - and
; `scripts/check-backtrace.sh` pins the frames, where it controls the
; optimisation level and can compare all eight byte for byte.
;
; THE DIVISOR COMES FROM `sysArgc`. A literal zero folds at
; compile time and the guard the emitter writes is never reached, so a
; constant here would make this case pass by not testing anything -
; the same trap `314-out-of-memory` records for its own size.
(import IO)

(import Sys)

(pub :: depth3 (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (depth3 n) (/ 100 n))

(pub :: depth2 (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (depth2 n) (+ 1 (depth3 n)))

(pub :: depth1 (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (depth1 n) (+ 1 (depth2 n)))

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    (printlnLit (strCStr "dividing by a zero the compiler cannot see"))
    (depth1 (- sysArgc 1))
  }
)
